### Atlas S11 — compliance & rule-pack plugins by hand.
### Set @token from a register-org / login response, and @worker to a hired worker's id.

@base = http://localhost:5080/api/v1
@token = PASTE_A_SESSION_TOKEN
@worker = PASTE_A_WORKER_ID

### A worker's compliance rollup — required documents come from THEIR country's rule pack.
### A brand-new worker is non-compliant: every required document is Missing.
GET {{base}}/workers/{{worker}}/compliance
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### Record a required document (metadata + optional expiry; never the file itself).
POST {{base}}/workers/{{worker}}/documents
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "kind": "WorkAuthorization",
  "reference": "permit-2026-00417",
  "expiresOn": "2027-03-31"
}

### An unknown kind is a 400 (parse-and-reject, not a silent default).
POST {{base}}/workers/{{worker}}/documents
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "kind": "NotARealKind", "reference": "x" }

### List a worker's recorded documents.
GET {{base}}/workers/{{worker}}/documents
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### The org-wide expiring-documents view — what lapses in the next N days.
GET {{base}}/compliance/expiring?withinDays=30
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
### The thesis, by hand: a JPN or CAN worker is priced/complied the instant the
### pack file exists — no registry, calculator, or endpoint change. Hire a worker
### in "JPN" and quote them: a high earner's quote carries the family-support levy
### line that lives ONLY in JapanRulePack.cs. A "CAN" worker uses pure data.
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
